    "q-86868": {
      "dom_id": "step-q86868",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Find  ${lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-1)+1}{(-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x(x+y)+(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+1)(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y} & =\\frac{(-1)+1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
